This page lists the commands of `@micacms/editor` (the local editor) and of the self-hosted image. A command or option without a version exists since 0.2.0.

## The local editor

Run each command with `npx @micacms/editor <command>`. When `@micacms/editor` is installed in the project, `npx mica <command>` is the same. `npx @micacms/editor --help` shows the same list.

The content root is the path that you give, or `MICA_CONTENT_ROOT`, or the directory with a `.mica/schema.json`, or `./content`, in this order. Mica looks for `.mica/schema.json` at most three levels below the current directory, and not in `node_modules` or a hidden directory. If it finds more than one, give the path.

### start

```bash
npx mica start [content dir]
```

Opens the editor on this machine. It has no sign-in, and it listens only on loopback.

| Option | Default | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `-c`, `--content <dir>` | Found by its `.mica/schema.json` | The content root. A path without a flag does the same. |
| `-p`, `--port <number>` | `PORT`, else `6422` | The port to listen on. |
| `--host <address>` | `HOST`, else `127.0.0.1` | The address to bind. |
| `--open`, `--no-open` | open in an interactive terminal, not in CI | Opens the browser, or does not. |
| `-h`, `--help` | | Shows the help. |
| `-v`, `--version` | | Shows the version. |

`start` stops with exit code 64 when the content directory does not exist, and with 78 when `.mica/schema.json` is missing. Run `npx astro sync` to write the model.

### init

Added in 0.5.0.

```bash
npx mica init
```

Adds Mica to the Astro project in the current directory. It writes a starter model in `src/mica.ts` and the collections in `src/content.config.ts`. Then it tells you which scripts (`mica`, `check`) to set in `package.json`. It never overwrites a file. It takes no options.

### check

Added in 0.5.0.

```bash
npx mica check [content dir]
```

Checks every entry against the model: fields that do not validate, missing media, relations to entries that do not exist, and files that Mica does not read. It prints each problem. Use it in CI as `npm run check` or `npx --no mica check`: when `@micacms/editor` is missing, for example because CI installs no development dependencies, a plain `npx mica` can download and run an unrelated package called `mica`.

### import

Added in 0.6.0.

```bash
npx mica import [names] [options]
```

Moves Astro content collections into Mica, from Astro's data store. Without names, it imports every collection that the model defines. See [Add Mica to an existing site](/docs/existing-site/).

| Option | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `--from <file>` | Astro's data store. Default: `node_modules/.astro/data-store.json`, else `.astro/data-store.json`. |
| `-c`, `--content <dir>` | The content root. |
| `--map <c.field=key>` | Takes a Mica field from another key. `@body` is the Markdown body. Use it more than once. |
| `--order <c=field>` | The first order of a sortable collection, by a field. |

### Exit codes

| Code | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `0` | Done. `check` found no error. |
| `1` | `check` found an error or no model, `import` failed, or `start` could not start, for example because the port is in use. |
| `64` | Wrong use: an unknown command or option, a missing value, no content directory, or `init` cannot run here. |
| `78` | Configuration: `start` found no content model, a model that it cannot read, or a setting that the local editor does not allow, such as a `HOST` that is not on this computer. |

## The self-hosted image

Without a command, or with `serve`, the image serves the editor. `check` tests the configuration, and `compact` makes the clone small again. Another command exits with 64.

### check

Added in 0.5.0.

```bash
docker compose run --rm mica check
```

Tests the configuration in a temporary clone, while the server can run:

- the configuration loads;
- the identity provider answers discovery, with the configured issuer. The line also shows the redirect URI to register;
- the token can read the branch;
- the token may push (a dry run, which cannot see branch protection or server hooks);
- the content root, and its model at `.mica/schema.json` loads;
- Git LFS is not used under the content root;
- the data volume is writable and has a layout that this version knows. If there is a clone, the line shows its head, the number of drafts, a commit that is not pushed, and work that the next start finishes.

It prints one line for each check, `ok` or `FAIL`, and exits with 0 when all pass, 1 when a check fails, and 78 when the configuration does not load. The settings are in [Configuration](/docs/configuration/). The endpoints are in [Health endpoints](/docs/reference-health/).

### compact

```bash
docker compose stop mica
docker compose run --rm mica compact
docker compose start mica
```

Makes a new clone on the data volume, with only the current files. The clone grows with each media change on the website, and a new clone is often much smaller. It refuses a clone with drafts or with a publication that is not pushed yet: start Mica, publish or discard them, and stop it again. While another Mica uses the volume, it waits up to 45 seconds, then refuses. It exits with 0 when it compacted the clone or found none, 1 when the clone holds work, and 78 when the configuration does not load or another Mica uses the volume.
